#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119939 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119510 :question/id #uuid "9bd80a60-0f80-5cc2-8193-86fb32c79913" :question/math-academy-id "e-12467" :question/problem "If $x=2y^2 + \\dfrac1{y^2}$, then find $\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm dx}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(2{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(2{y}^{2}+{y}^{-2}) \\\\  & =2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{-2}) \\\\  & =4y-2{y}^{-3} \\\\  & =4y-\\frac{2}{{y}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{y}^{4}-2}{{y}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\dfrac{\\textrm dx}{\\textrm dy},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm dx} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\textrm dx}{\\textrm dy} \\right)^{-1} = \\dfrac{y^3}{4y^4 -2}.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "00ac6edc-908a-50be-9ef1-4b24ba597d1d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048932 :topic/id #uuid "cd2e9db4-6727-5887-90bc-20eb0a0bf6d2" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059862} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059863} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059864} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059865}] :topic/math-academy-id 278 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048442} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050711}] :topic/title "The Sum and Constant Multiple Rules for Differentiation"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050310 :topic/encompasses [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429224 :encompassing/rationale "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account foundations: 1 rises / 1 eligible passed source activities = 1.000000; nearest selected fraction = 1. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: foundations=1 (1/1). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Interveni